{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Multimedia Storyboarding and AI Asset Generation","title":"Module 3 Storyboard Studio","description":"The Module 3 capstone studio: integrating seven prior storyboard skills into one coherent frame sequence, auditing objective trace, claim chain, and principle consistency across frames, and shipping an auditable decision record of accepted and rejected (including AI-suggested) choices.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-01","statement":"Audit a multi-frame storyboard for sequence coherence (objective trace, claim chain, principle consistency) and ship an auditable decision record of accepted and rejected choices in AIL 606"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation","ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"08","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/03/07"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_ai_reject","ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_ai_reject"],"ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Module 3 Storyboard Studio\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Module 3 Storyboard Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/03-08-module-3-storyboard-studio.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Module 3 Storyboard Studio: Module 3 Storyboard Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\nThe seven prior sections each taught a strong move in isolation: frames as claims, signaling and segmenting, image commissioning, modality, accessibility, prompt logs, and peer review. The studio is where they have to work together, and integration is its own skill, distinct from any of the parts. A storyboard that staples seven good techniques onto separate frames is not a designed sequence; it is a portfolio of demonstrations. The capstone failure is not a weak frame but an incoherent whole, individually polished frames that do not build toward one objective and that apply principles inconsistently across the sequence.\n\nThis section treats the studio as an exercise in coherence across frames plus an accountable decision record. Coherence means every frame traces to one objective and the prior-state-to-action chain actually links frame to frame, so a principle honored on Frame 2 is not abandoned on Frame 4. The decision record means the integrated storyboard ships with a short rationale, key choices, rejected options, and warrants, so a reviewer can audit the sequence as a single argument rather than admiring frames one at a time.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA team assembles its capstone storyboard the night before it is due. Frame 1 reuses the polished claim from 03.01; Frame 3 drops in the verified lever diagram from 03.03 with its alt text from 03.05 pasted underneath; the peer comments from 03.07 are stapled to the end. Each piece is genuinely good. But Frame 2 gives the learner a Continue control while Frame 4 auto-advances through three dense steps, the segmenting principle honored in one place and broken in another, and no thread connects the frames toward a single objective.\n\nA reviewer reading frame by frame would praise each panel. A reviewer reading the sequence sees a stapled collection with an internal contradiction. The strong frames hide the weak whole. This section asks you to expose the seam: the studio is graded on whether the sequence is one coherent argument toward an objective, not on whether each frame is individually impressive.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nIntegration rests on two ideas that do not appear when you work one frame at a time.\n\nThe first is **sequence coherence**. A storyboard is coherent when every frame serves one objective and the chain of claims actually links: the predicted action of Frame N becomes the prior state of Frame N+1 (the discipline from 03.01 applied across the whole sequence). Incoherence shows up as a gap (two frames with no connecting step), a redundancy (two frames making the same move), or an inconsistency (a principle applied here and abandoned there). Coherence is checked by reading the sequence as a path, not by inspecting frames in isolation.\n\nThe second is the **decision record** as the unit of evidence. At capstone scale, no reviewer can re-derive every choice from the frames alone, so the storyboard ships with a one-page record: the major decisions, the options rejected, and the warrant for each. The record is what makes a long artifact auditable, and it is where the seven prior skills become legible as deliberate choices rather than lucky defaults.\n\nThe course anchors now function as a consistency audit across the whole. Mayer's principles must be applied the same way in comparable frames (segmenting on Frame 2 implies segmenting on Frame 4). Cognitive load theory governs the accumulation of load across the sequence, not just within a frame, because a learner's working memory carries over. UDL learner variability is checked end to end: the named target learner must be able to traverse the entire path, not merely succeed on the easiest frame. A principle that holds frame by frame but contradicts itself across frames signals that the parts were assembled, not integrated.\n\n## Worked Example: Integrating a Module-3 Storyboard\n\nThe studio task is a five-frame storyboard teaching one objective: the learner can predict the next stage of the water cycle. Below is the before/after of the capstone as a whole.\n\n**Before (a collection of strong but disconnected pieces):**\n\n> Frame 1 reuses the polished claim from 03.01. Frame 3 drops in the verified lever-style diagram skill from 03.03. The alt text from 03.05 is pasted under it. The peer comments from 03.07 are attached at the end. No connecting rationale. Frame 2 has a Continue control; Frame 4 auto-advances through three dense steps.\n\nProblems: each piece is good in isolation, but the sequence has an inconsistency (segmenting honored on Frame 2, broken on Frame 4) and reads as a stapled collection, not an integrated storyboard with one objective.\n\n**After (integrated toward the objective, with a decision record):**\n\n> All five frames trace one objective: setup (name the stages), claim frames (predict the next stage), evidence frame (check the prediction). Segmenting is applied consistently: every frame ends on a learner-paced Continue. Modality, image, and alt-text choices each cite the principle and the objective they serve. A one-page decision record lists the major choices, the rejected options, and the warrant for each.\n\nAnnotated decision trail:\n\n1. Traced the objective across all five frames and closed the gap so the sequence builds to the prediction, instead of optimizing frames in isolation.\n2. Made segmenting consistent: fixed Frame 4 to match Frame 2's learner pacing.\n3. Integrated, did not collect: each reused skill (claim, image, alt text, review) is justified by how it serves this one objective.\n4. Attached the decision record: key choices, rejected options (for example, an AI-suggested sixth bonus frame, rejected for diluting the objective), and the principle or learner reasoning behind each.\n\nThe improvement is one coherent storyboard with a visible evidence trail, not a folder of strong but unconnected artifacts.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nAudit the storyboard as a whole before judging any frame, using the integration sequence:\n\n1. **Objective trace:** Walk every frame and ask \"how does this serve the one objective?\" A frame that serves no objective, or a second objective, is the seam to fix; cut it or fold it in.\n2. **Chain check:** Confirm Frame N's predicted action is Frame N+1's prior state. Gaps and redundancies both break the chain and both need a revision.\n3. **Consistency sweep:** Pick one principle (segmenting, modality, accessibility) and check it is applied the same way in every comparable frame. Inconsistency is the signature of assembly, not integration.\n4. **Record check:** Does a one-page decision record list the major choices, the rejected options, and a warrant for each? If a reviewer would have to ask you, the record is incomplete.\n\nA capstone passes when the sequence reads as one argument and the record lets a reviewer audit it without you in the room.\n\nOrder the integration audit the way you would run it on a finished capstone.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Objective trace: confirm every frame serves the one objective\",\"Chain check: verify Frame N action becomes Frame N+1 prior state\",\"Consistency sweep: check one principle is applied the same way across comparable frames\",\"Record check: confirm the one-page record lists choices, rejected options, and warrants\",\"Judge the whole as one argument, not frame by frame\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nWalk the stapled water-cycle capstone toward integration.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Integrating the capstone, one audit at a time\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Objective: the learner can predict the next stage of the water cycle. An AI tool suggested a sixth bonus frame on cloud types. Objective trace: keep it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep it; more content enriches the module\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Cloud types do not serve the prediction objective. A frame that serves a second objective is the seam to fix.\"},{\"label\":\"Cut it; it dilutes the one objective\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: integration is mostly subtraction. Cut what does not serve the objective.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"The reviewer says the sequence wanders. What did the objective trace catch?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A frame serving a different goal, which should be cut or folded in\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right: every frame must trace to the single objective.\"}],\"feedback\":\"A strong frame that serves the wrong objective still fails the whole.\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"Chain check passes once the stray frame is gone. Now the consistency sweep on segmenting: Frame 2 ends on a learner Continue, but Frame 4 auto-advances through three dense steps. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Leave it; each frame was individually polished\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Inconsistency is the signature of assembly, not integration. A principle honored on Frame 2 cannot be abandoned on Frame 4.\"},{\"label\":\"Fix Frame 4 to end on a learner Continue like Frame 2\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: comparable frames must behave alike for the sequence to be coherent.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer reading the sequence flags a contradiction. What principle did the sweep expose?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Segmenting must be applied the same way across comparable frames\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Right: load accumulates across the sequence, so consistency is graded at the level of the whole.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Individually polished frames can still contradict each other.\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"The sequence is coherent. What ships with it so a reviewer can audit it without you?\",\"feedback\":\"A one-page decision record: the major choices, the rejected options (including the cut bonus frame and the Frame 4 segmenting fix), and a warrant for each reused skill. At capstone scale the record is part of the design, not paperwork added after it.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that a strong capstone is the sum of strong frames. It is not; a sequence of excellent but disconnected frames fails on coherence, which is graded at the level of the whole. The second misreading is that the decision record is paperwork added after the design. At capstone scale the record is part of the design, because it is the only thing that makes a multi-frame artifact auditable; a storyboard whose rationale lives only in the authors' heads cannot be reviewed or revised by anyone else, including a teammate next week.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A capstone has five individually excellent frames, but Frame 2 ends on a learner Continue while Frame 4 auto-advances through three dense steps. Why does the sequence still fail?\" explanation=\"Coherence is graded at the level of the whole, not the frame. Segmenting honored on Frame 2 and abandoned on Frame 4 is an inconsistency, the signature of assembly rather than integration, and load accumulates across the sequence. Strong frames cannot rescue a sequence that contradicts itself.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It passes, because each frame is individually strong\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It fails on coherence: a principle is applied inconsistently across comparable frames\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It fails only because there are too few frames\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk the stapled water-cycle capstone toward integration. The objective is \"the learner can predict the next stage of the water cycle.\" Run the objective trace: Frames 1 and 3 serve it, but an AI tool suggested a sixth bonus frame on cloud types that does not, so it is cut for diluting the objective. Run the chain check: Frame 1 names the stages (prior state), the claim frames have the learner predict the next stage (action), and an evidence frame checks the prediction, so the chain links once Frame 5's stray content is removed. Run the consistency sweep on segmenting: Frame 2 ends on a learner Continue but Frame 4 auto-advances, so Frame 4 is fixed to match, removing the contradiction. Finally, attach a decision record listing the cut bonus frame, the segmenting fix, and the warrant for each reused skill.\n\nThe result is not seven trophies on a shelf; it is one coherent storyboard that builds to a prediction, applies its principles consistently, and ships with a record a reviewer can audit. The walkthrough shows integration is mostly subtraction and alignment, cutting what does not serve the objective and making comparable frames behave alike, not adding more.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead the capstone as a path, not a gallery. Trace the objective through every frame and flag the first frame that serves a different goal; follow the prior-state-to-action chain and flag the first broken link; pick one principle and flag the first inconsistency. A strong reader resists being charmed by an individually beautiful frame and instead asks whether it earns its place in the sequence. Read the decision record adversarially: for each major choice, ask whether the stated warrant would actually survive a reviewer's challenge.\n\nA strong reader of an integrated storyboard can name its single objective, point to any broken link or inconsistency in the chain, and judge whether the decision record makes the whole auditable without the author.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact reaches the stronger standard when the sequence is coherent end to end and ships with an auditable decision record. Every frame traces to one objective, the claim chain links without gaps or redundancies, at least one principle is verifiably applied the same way across comparable frames, and the record lists major choices, rejected options (including at least one tool suggestion declined for diluting the objective), and a warrant each. Reused skills from earlier sections appear as justified choices, not pasted fragments.\n\nA strong closing note reads: \"I cut the AI-suggested bonus frame because it served cloud types, not the prediction objective. I fixed Frame 4 to end on a learner Continue so segmenting is consistent with Frame 2. Each reused skill, the claim frame, the verified image, the alt text, the peer-review record, is justified by how it serves the one objective, and the attached decision record lets a reviewer audit the sequence without asking me to narrate it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the integration discipline from a single self-paced module to a multi-week course where this storyboard is one unit among several, built by rotating team members. Coherence now operates at two scales: within the storyboard and across units that must not contradict each other or repeat the same move, and the decision record becomes a handoff document so the next team can extend the design without re-deriving it. The transfer test is whether you can keep the objective-trace and consistency-sweep discipline when the \"whole\" is a course, not a module, and when the auditor is a future teammate rather than an instructor. If your integration skill only works on artifacts you built alone and finished in one sitting, it does not scale; the durable skill is making coherence and rationale legible to people who arrive after you.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/03-08-module-3-storyboard-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"03.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 03.08. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Module 3 Storyboard Studio, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how the capstone studio integrates the chapter: storyboarding, evidence-based revision, and bounded AI support converge on one auditable sequence.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_multimedia_storyboarding_and_ai_asset_generation\",\"ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio","stem":"A studio submission has eight beautifully polished frames, but a reviewer notes the sequence skips from defining photosynthesis straight to a quiz with no worked step between. What does this reveal about the storyboard?","options":["Nothing; each frame is high quality, so the storyboard is strong.","The frames were optimized in isolation; the full sequence has a gap and does not carry the learner to the objective.","The quiz should simply be removed.","The frames need brighter colors."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A studio submission is judged as a whole sequence. Excellent individual frames that leave a gap toward the objective still fail as a storyboard.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_frames_dont_need_to_cohere","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio","stem":"In one storyboard, Frame 2 gives the learner a Continue control to pace the content, but Frame 6 auto-advances through a dense multi-step procedure. What design flaw does this show?","options":["Frame 6 should also be removed.","A principle (segmenting) was applied inconsistently across the storyboard, signaling an unintegrated design.","Continue controls are never appropriate.","The two frames should use the same color."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Principles must hold across the whole storyboard. Honoring segmenting on one frame and breaking it on another shows the design was not integrated.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_principles_applied_once","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio","stem":"For the studio capstone, a learner pastes together the frame exercise, the image, the alt text, and the peer comments from earlier in the module with no connecting rationale. Why does this fall short of the studio's intent?","options":["The artifacts should have been done in a different order.","The studio integrates the pieces into one defensible storyboard serving a single objective; a collection is not an integration.","Earlier artifacts may not be reused at all.","The capstone should contain only new work."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The capstone asks the learner to integrate frame, modality, accessibility, and review decisions toward one objective, not to staple separate exercises together.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_studio_is_just_assembly","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio","stem":"A learner submits a polished final storyboard for the studio with no other materials. Which addition would most raise the quality of the capstone as evidence of learning?","options":["A higher-resolution export of the same storyboard.","A decision record listing the major design choices, the options rejected, and the principle or learner data behind each one.","A longer title slide.","More frames of the same kind."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The capstone needs its evidence trail. A decision record of accepted and rejected choices, with warrants, is what turns a polished storyboard into defensible evidence of design judgment.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_revision_trail_optional","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_module_3_storyboard_studio"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_module_3_storyboard_studio_ai_reject","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"Your capstone objective is 'the learner can predict the next stage of the water cycle.' An AI tool suggests a sixth bonus frame on cloud types, and it is genuinely well designed. What is the defensible move?","options":["Accept it; a well-designed frame always strengthens the sequence.","Accept it but place it last so it cannot interrupt the chain.","Reject it and record the reason in the decision record: it serves a second objective (cloud types), not the prediction objective, so it dilutes the sequence.","Reject it without documentation, since rejected suggestions do not belong in the package."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The objective trace governs AI suggestions just like your own ideas: every frame must serve the single stated objective. A polished frame serving a different goal is exactly the seam that makes a capstone an assembly rather than an integration. The bounded AI move is to reject it and preserve the rejection, with its reason, in the decision record a reviewer can audit.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_ai_suggestion_accepted_uncritically","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-01","ail606-supplement-03-08-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_frames_dont_need_to_cohere","pattern":"frames_optimized_in_isolation","description":"Polishing individual frames well but never checking that the full storyboard tells one coherent instructional sequence toward the objective","trigger":"learner perfects single frames but the storyboard has gaps or repeats across the sequence","feedback":"A studio submission is the whole sequence. Trace the objective across all frames; strong frames that do not connect still fail the storyboard.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_principles_applied_once","pattern":"principle_applied_inconsistently","description":"Applying a multimedia principle on one frame but contradicting it elsewhere in the same storyboard","trigger":"learner segments one frame but lets a later frame auto-advance through dense content","feedback":"Apply each principle consistently across the storyboard. A principle honored on Frame 2 and broken on Frame 6 signals an unintegrated design.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_studio_is_just_assembly","pattern":"studio_treated_as_collection","description":"Treating the studio capstone as collecting the module's separate artifacts rather than integrating them into one defensible storyboard with an evidence trail","trigger":"learner staples together earlier exercises with no integrating rationale","feedback":"The studio integrates, it does not collect. Show how the frames, modality, accessibility, and review decisions serve one objective.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_revision_trail_optional","pattern":"capstone_without_decision_record","description":"Submitting a final storyboard with no record of the major design decisions, rejected options, and the evidence behind them","trigger":"learner submits the polished storyboard alone with no decision log","feedback":"The capstone needs its evidence trail: key decisions, what was rejected, and the principle or learner data behind each choice.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_3_8_ai_suggestion_accepted_uncritically","pattern":"ai_suggestion_kept_because_plausible","description":"Accepting an AI-suggested addition (such as a bonus frame) because it looks good or enriches the content, rather than because it serves the storyboard's single objective.","trigger":"learner keeps an AI-suggested frame that introduces a second objective into the sequence","feedback":"Run the objective trace on every AI suggestion: a frame that does not serve the one stated objective dilutes the sequence, however polished it is. Integration is mostly subtraction; cut the addition and record the rejection with its reason in the decision record.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"3e1d29713d09a3bbb692295c8e590d56d737262aac693368ac8209a9b75e521e","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}